A||N||12||bHooper||R134 conversion ||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||08-08-2000||01:42 PM||||at lunch today, i bought a R134 conversion kit.  it is made by <B>interdynamics</B> and came from AutoZone.<P>the only conversion required is to evacuate and recharge the system!<P>anyone with any experience with these types of kits?<P>also, i am missing the bolt that attach's the lines to the compressor (v-6 car), does anyone know if this is a standard bolt that i can get from NAPA?<P>thanks<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||08-08-2000||02:44 PM||||When I converted mine to R-134 a couple of years ago I also replaced the reciever/dryer.<BR>It's that silver can up front.  I also drained as much oil as possible out of the system since the 134 is designed to work with a different type of oil.  That said, I know of several folks on this forum that have done nothing more than evacuate and recharge after installing the service fitting adapters, and I haven't heard of any problems from them, so maybe that's all you need to do.<BR>I can't help on the bolt question, mines a 4 cylinder.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||08-08-2000||03:12 PM||||You can get the hardware at any scrap yard. Maybe at napa or a hardware store.<P>If your system has been left open to the air you MUST replace the ACC/Dryer can and the orifice tube as well as any O rings you expose changing those parts. The Acc/dryer will draw it's full capacity of water in no time. You can't just vac out that much water.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||08-08-2000||04:07 PM||||I would guess that the folks who live in northern states and use A/C sparingly are probably the ones who can use the kit with minimal labor and have few, if any problems.<P>And they probably make sense if you have a beater and it isn't cost effective or sensible to have an A/C system that's worth more than the car it's attached to.<P>But given the relatively low cost of parts for these cars (orifice tube $2 or less, acc/dryer maybe $30), it makes sense to replace these, especially if they're the originals. <P>But if you want a retrofit that lasts, change these and the hoses in the engine compartment too. I recently replaced all the components on my A/C and charged it up only to find I had a leak at one of the crimps on my A/C hose.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||08-08-2000||04:09 PM||||the system has been open since april 1st.<P>the gm part number for the bolt is #1150931, 10x1.5x35 metric and is discontinued.  i can get a 10x1.5x30 at AutoZone.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
