Z||000008||bHooper||08-10-2000||06:46 AM||||pk, so here is the update.  the A/C worked great!  cooling ok in the carolina heat.<P>until... the compressor went out last night!  man what a horible sound... kinda like some animal dyeing!  anyway, this sucker seized up last night.<P>which brings me to a question:  can you rebuild the compressor, or is this not a good idea?  one day of A/C in my little sportscar spoiled me!  <P>i guess ill have to drive my jeep today!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||08-10-2000||08:24 AM||||All of the 'good ole boy' auto parts houses (the kind I've learned to trust) warned me about using re-built compressors when converting to R-134a.  When I put my new engine in I replaced my siezed up DA-6 with a new AC Delco one.  I replaced the drier and orifice tube and replaced every o-ring with new ones that came in the conversion kit.  While I had the lines cracked open I flushed each section (and the condenser) using the flush fluid and compressed air.<P>I've had it in about a year and it is great (even in Birmingham, Alabama with two weeks of over 100 degree temps this summer).<P>Replacing all the o-rings wasn't as bad as I thought it would be.  While the engine was out I disconnected the hoses from the steel lines that run under the car.  I then removed the spare tire tub (be sure to unhook the brake line support clips from underneath) and disconnected the steel lines where they attach to the drier.  Last was where the lines attach to the condenser behind the grill on the driver's side.<P>I don't notice any loss of performance in the system.  I did my 89 Bonneville about 4 years ago and it is still going strong (used original compressor, did not replace o-rings) and it is still working great.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||olympic||08-10-2000||01:45 PM||||  The reason why some people blow compressors and seals after converting to R134 is because it runs at nearly 3 times the pressure of R12.  That is why most shops will replace the compressor if the car is pre 1990 when converting.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodrv6||08-10-2000||03:49 PM||||3 times the pressure?!?!?  It's really more like 10-15% more than R-12.  My high side pressure only went up about 20 psi over the R-12 readings.  If you are getting 3 times the pressure over R-12, then there is WAY too much refrigerant in the system!  It is true that an old compressor that is nearly worn out may not last long with the 134.  I have had excellent results with a rebuilt DA-6 compressor on my system.  It's been several years since the conversion and all is well.  I can still make icicles in the Georgia summer heat.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||5
Z||000012||bHooper||08-10-2000||10:20 PM||||well, as sorry as i am to say it... it was probably time for the compressor to go.  the thing hadnt been used in 4 years.  the system had only been disconected for 4 months.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
